{
 "turns": [
  {
   "idx": 0,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Good morning, Mrs. Turner. Can you tell me your full name and your birthday?",
   "start": 0.05,
   "end": 4.25
  },
  {
   "idx": 1,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "Odessa Turner. April 19, 1935. I'm 91 years old and I still remember",
   "start": 5.11,
   "end": 11.77
  },
  {
   "idx": 2,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "it, so don't you worry. Sharp as ever, did Gloria bring you today?",
   "start": 11.77,
   "end": 15.99
  },
  {
   "idx": 3,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "She did. She's out in the waiting room on her phone. She's the one who made me come. Well, I'm glad she did.",
   "start": 16.77,
   "end": 22.91
  },
  {
   "idx": 4,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "What's been going on?",
   "start": 23.17,
   "end": 24.15
  },
  {
   "idx": 5,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "I'm just worn out, Doctor, for about six weeks now. I nap in the morning and again after lunch, and I used to never nap. My tomatoes are going to waste because I can't get out to the garden.",
   "start": 24.75,
   "end": 35.99
  },
  {
   "idx": 6,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "That's a big change for you, any dizziness.",
   "start": 36.85,
   "end": 38.95
  },
  {
   "idx": 7,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "When I first get up out of bed in the morning, the room kind of swims for a second. Then it passes.",
   "start": 40.13,
   "end": 45.53
  },
  {
   "idx": 8,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Have you fallen at all?",
   "start": 46.6,
   "end": 47.9
  },
  {
   "idx": 9,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "No, I hold on to the dresser.",
   "start": 48.92,
   "end": 50.66
  },
  {
   "idx": 10,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Any chest pain or shortness of breath?",
   "start": 51.08,
   "end": 53.4
  },
  {
   "idx": 11,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "No chest pain, I breathe fine.",
   "start": 54.24,
   "end": 56.3
  },
  {
   "idx": 12,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Fever, black stools, or losing weight without trying.",
   "start": 56.3,
   "end": 60.1
  },
  {
   "idx": 13,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "No, none of that.",
   "start": 60.9,
   "end": 61.74
  },
  {
   "idx": 14,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Have you been feeling down or sad?",
   "start": 62.62,
   "end": 64.54
  },
  {
   "idx": 15,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "No, I'm not sad. Just tired.",
   "start": 65.43,
   "end": 67.25
  },
  {
   "idx": 16,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Let's go through your pills. I have amlodipine 5 milligrams once a day for your blood pressure, metoprolol 50 milligrams twice a day, atorvastatin 10 at night, and a baby aspirin 81 milligrams. Anything else?",
   "start": 67.91,
   "end": 79.93
  },
  {
   "idx": 17,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "That's right. And I take a Tylenol PM at bedtime, because I wasn't sleeping. Gloria bought me a big bottle about two months ago.",
   "start": 80.51,
   "end": 88.61
  },
  {
   "idx": 18,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Every night?",
   "start": 89.33,
   "end": 89.69
  },
  {
   "idx": 19,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "Every night. Oh, and I quit the vitamin D. Those pills are too big to swallow. Okay, that's helpful.",
   "start": 90.43,
   "end": 97.41
  },
  {
   "idx": 20,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Any other new problems this year?",
   "start": 97.73,
   "end": 99.33
  },
  {
   "idx": 21,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "Well, I had pneumonia last winter, but I got over that. Lungs have been fine since.",
   "start": 100.28,
   "end": 105.28
  },
  {
   "idx": 22,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Good, let me check you. Sitting, your blood pressure is 118 over 64 and your pulse is 52, which is on the slow side. Standing up, the pressure drops to 104 over 60. Your heart is slow but regular, no murmur. Lungs are clear, no swelling in your ankles. Your color looks good, no paleness inside the eyelids. We also did an ECG while you were waiting. It shows a slow, regular rhythm, a rate of 52, and no heart block.",
   "start": 106.02,
   "end": 133.01
  },
  {
   "idx": 23,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "So what's making me so tired?",
   "start": 133.87,
   "end": 135.23
  },
  {
   "idx": 24,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "I think a big part is your medicines. Your pulse is slow and the metoprolol may be slowing it too much, and the Tylenol PM has diphenhydramine in it, which is a sleepy antihistamine. At 91, it can make you drowsy all day.",
   "start": 136.06,
   "end": 148.88
  },
  {
   "idx": 25,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "My goodness, I thought it was just the Tylenol.",
   "start": 149.38,
   "end": 151.94
  },
  {
   "idx": 26,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "A lot of people think that, so here's the plan. Stop the Tylenol PM. If you have aches at night, plain Tylenol that's acetaminophen 500 mg is fine. Cut the metoprolol down to 25 milligrams twice a day, that's half a tablet. Keep the amlodipine, atorvastatin, and aspirin the same.",
   "start": 152.42,
   "end": 169.72
  },
  {
   "idx": 27,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "What about sleeping then?",
   "start": 170.3,
   "end": 171.18
  },
  {
   "idx": 28,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Same bedtime every night, no long afternoon naps, and some light in the morning. That helps more than you'd think. For the vitamin D, I'll switch you to a chewable 1 ,000 units a day.",
   "start": 171.78,
   "end": 181.6
  },
  {
   "idx": 29,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "A chewable I can do.",
   "start": 182.14,
   "end": 183.14
  },
  {
   "idx": 30,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "I also want some blood work today, a blood count, chemistry panel, thyroid test, and B12 to make sure we're not missing anything else.",
   "start": 183.64,
   "end": 191.56
  },
  {
   "idx": 31,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "all right get up slowly sit on the",
   "start": 192.19,
   "end": 195.11
  },
  {
   "idx": 32,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "edge of the bed for a minute first if you faint have chest pain or your pulse gets slower call us or go to the emergency room I'll see you in two weeks and we'll call Gloria at 555 -0177 with the results that's her number thank you doctor I'll",
   "start": 195.11,
   "end": 210.87
  },
  {
   "idx": 33,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "bring you some tomatoes if I get my energy",
   "start": 210.87,
   "end": 212.91
  },
  {
   "idx": 34,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "back I'll hold you to that mrs. Turner",
   "start": 212.91,
   "end": 215.85
  }
 ],
